*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
* flag = !OSREAD(data, path)
*
* SYSTEM(1071) (SYS$OSREAD.INFO) then gives the STATUS array of the file that was read, from the named common sd.osreadinfo that
* _system reads; it is null before any OSREAD and after one that failed.
*
* Reads the whole of the operating system file PATH into DATA with no change to a byte (no newline is turned into a field mark),
* and gives 1; gives 0 and leaves DATA null if the file cannot be opened or read.  A file larger than a gigabyte is not read.
*
* THIS IS AN ORDINARY PROGRAM, NOT AN INTERNAL ONE, ON PURPOSE: it is made of OPENSEQ, which asks the OS-access gate of the account
* it runs in (S.63), so an account without the gate reaches only its own directory by OSREAD, as it does by OPENSEQ, and one
* with it reaches what the user can.  Making it internal would exempt it from the gate.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

function !osread(data, path)
$catalog !osread

   common /sd.osreadinfo/ osread.info    ;* what SYSTEM(1071) gives: _system reads it

   data = ''
   osread.info = ''
   if path = '' then return 0

   openseq path readonly to f then null else return 0

   total = 0
   loop
      readblk block from f, 32768 else exit
      total += len(block)
      if total > 1073741824 then
         closeseq f
         data = ''
         return 0
      end
      data := block
   repeat

   * The STATUS array of the file, as for a directory file item: the fields that are about reading a sequential file (position,
   * end of file, bytes left) mean nothing for an OSREAD that has read it all, and are null.
   status info from f then null else info = ''
   info<1> = ''
   info<2> = ''
   info<4> = ''
   osread.info = info

   closeseq f
   return 1
end

* END-CODE
